WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.250
YUFENG GUO: Previously
on AI Adventures,

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:06.060
we looked at how to use feature
activations for understanding

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:07.500
neural networks.

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:10.950
How can we extend these ideas
further and get even more out

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:12.120
of these methods?

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:13.650
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:16.968
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:26.570
Welcome to AI Adventures where
we explore the art, science,

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:28.790
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:28.790 --> 00:00:30.010
My name is Yufeng Guo.

00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:32.380
And on this episode,
we're going to extend

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:35.980
the ideas of feature activations
in convolutional neural

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:40.720
networks even further by
applying them to entire images

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:43.960
to create activation
grids and then see

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:49.060
how we can use that to
produce an activation atlas.

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:51.730
This video picks up where
the previous video left off.

00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:54.580
So if you haven't watched
that, you should definitely

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:56.050
check it out first.

00:00:56.050 --> 00:00:58.330
Now previously, we
looked at how the neurons

00:00:58.330 --> 00:01:00.700
in a convolutional
neural networks layers

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:03.580
can be organized into
channels and then stacked

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:05.480
like a layer cake.

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:08.920
We can also organize it
in our mind vertically

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:12.040
so that we are looking at a
certain patch of that layer

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:15.640
and consider the activation of
all neurons stacked together

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:16.870
over a region.

00:01:16.870 --> 00:01:19.980
We call this
spatial activations.

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:22.150
Spatial activations
give us a sense

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:25.240
of what features a
specific region of an image

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:27.850
is activating most strongly.

00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:31.570
By doing this across all the
different patches of an image,

00:01:31.570 --> 00:01:34.930
we can build an activation
grid, showing us

00:01:34.930 --> 00:01:38.530
the features that are activating
the strongest for each region.

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:41.200
This is for only one
particular layer.

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:44.890
So now we want to repeat this
process for the other layers--

00:01:44.890 --> 00:01:48.130
some earlier in the
network and some later.

00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:50.710
Earlier layers,
like mixed 3a, they

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:53.560
show us the edges and patterns
that still mildly resemble

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:56.200
the original image,
while later layers

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:58.750
are much more identifiable
as the animal body

00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:01.870
parts that are being recognized
in that part of image.

00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:04.390
Now, activations are--
they're just numbers, right?

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:08.150
And so some activations, they
can be larger than others.

00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:12.880
So what we can now do is scale
the size of each of these grids

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:14.630
to the size of the activation.

00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:17.140
So that'll give us a sense
of the importance of a given

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:20.980
region of the image to that
layer based on how strongly

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:22.780
the network is activating.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:25.840
So now we can see which regions
are most strongly activated

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:28.690
per layer, and how these
activations are propagating

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:31.040
through each layer
of the network.

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:34.120
Now, this gives us a
sense of the saliency

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:36.580
of the different
parts of the image.

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:38.800
Activation grids
tell us about some

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:42.620
of the activations of the neural
network for a specific image.

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:46.070
But what about all the other
activations of the network?

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:49.570
How might we understand the
other types of activations?

00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:52.930
Well, we'd need to
feed in lots of example

00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:55.430
images of all sorts of types.

00:02:55.430 --> 00:02:57.680
So what if we did that?

00:02:57.680 --> 00:02:59.650
We could run lots of images.

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:02.350
In fact, we could run a million
different example images

00:03:02.350 --> 00:03:06.640
through the network and record
all of their activation routes.

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:09.310
All these individual
activations correspond

00:03:09.310 --> 00:03:11.920
to some kind of
higher-dimensional location

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:16.580
in the neural networks
activation space, so to speak.

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:19.720
And if we position
our activations, each

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:22.720
of these subgrids, into
their respective locations

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:25.040
in this
higher-dimensional space,

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:28.000
we now have for our
1 million example

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:33.010
images broken up and scattered
across this landscape.

00:03:33.010 --> 00:03:35.260
Unfortunately,
high-dimensional spaces

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:37.430
are, well, they're tough
for us to visualize.

00:03:37.430 --> 00:03:41.290
So we can project this space
down to two dimensions.

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:42.990
But there's still
the problem having

00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:45.850
just millions of activations
that deal with array.

00:03:45.850 --> 00:03:49.100
So that's rather
unwieldy for visualizing.

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:53.500
So let's draw a grid over
the whole 2D projection.

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:56.170
And we'll take for
each grid the average

00:03:56.170 --> 00:03:59.060
of the activations within it.

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:02.080
This won't cause us to lose too
much detail since activations

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:05.240
that are close together,
they're similar to one another.

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:08.860
So now we can finally
resize each grid

00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:11.770
based on a number of
activations on our grid,

00:04:11.770 --> 00:04:14.530
giving us a sense of how
strongly the network responds

00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:18.430
to that particular
type of input.

00:04:18.430 --> 00:04:20.470
Now, we can repeat
this entire process

00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:22.690
for a couple different
layers, put it

00:04:22.690 --> 00:04:27.790
into a nice web interface, and
you have an activation atlas.

00:04:27.790 --> 00:04:30.280
So let's explore
it and see what it

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:34.450
looks like to navigate
through this space.

00:04:34.450 --> 00:04:39.226
If we start our journey in the
top area of the layer mixed 4c,

00:04:39.226 --> 00:04:43.000
there appears to be kind
of a variety of dog faces.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:46.000
Then as we go down, things
shift to showing how

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:48.670
the backs of
four-legged animals.

00:04:48.670 --> 00:04:50.080
And then we keep going.

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:54.220
We get to some legs and
feet merging into the ground

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:55.790
and ground-related things.

00:04:55.790 --> 00:05:00.580
And that slowly transitions
into kind of sandy services

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:02.230
and some water.

00:05:02.230 --> 00:05:04.870
There's lots of interesting pats
through the activation atlas.

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:08.560
And it's really neat, I
think, that the images shown,

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:13.570
they smoothly transition
from one region to the next.

00:05:13.570 --> 00:05:18.820
We can also look at a specific
class activations in the atlas

00:05:18.820 --> 00:05:21.610
so we can filter it out and just
look at one particular class.

00:05:21.610 --> 00:05:24.310
Let's say the great white shark.

00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:28.300
I like to start with the
bottom-most layer, mixed 5b,

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:29.980
since that's going
to show images that

00:05:29.980 --> 00:05:32.038
are most similar to kind
of the final prediction

00:05:32.038 --> 00:05:33.580
so we can really
recognize what it is

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:35.570
we're seeing-- it's meaningful.

00:05:35.570 --> 00:05:37.180
We see some regions
we'd expect--

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:38.980
various fish-like imagery.

00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:42.370
And over here there's some
water-related imagery, right?

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:44.920
And what's this over here?

00:05:44.920 --> 00:05:47.620
Yeah, is that a baseball?

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:52.490
What is a baseball doing so
late in the network structure?

00:05:52.490 --> 00:05:56.080
So if the network thinks that
baseballs have a pretty strong

00:05:56.080 --> 00:05:59.240
relationship to, well,
great white sharks,

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:00.730
what would happen then?

00:06:00.730 --> 00:06:02.920
Perhaps if we added an
image of a baseball,

00:06:02.920 --> 00:06:06.400
just slapped it on top of
this image of a gray whale,

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:10.330
could we fool our neural
network into thinking that it's

00:06:10.330 --> 00:06:12.610
seeing a great white shark?

00:06:12.610 --> 00:06:14.830
At first, it doesn't quite work.

00:06:14.830 --> 00:06:17.560
But with a bit of fiddling
around with the image size,

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:20.620
we can get the network to
output great white shark

00:06:20.620 --> 00:06:23.050
as its top prediction
for the image.

00:06:23.050 --> 00:06:23.980
This is pretty fun.

00:06:23.980 --> 00:06:26.980
And you can look for all
sorts of other oddities.

00:06:26.980 --> 00:06:30.400
I also found in the mixed 5b
layer for a great white shark

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:33.700
that airship is another kind
of unusual label associated

00:06:33.700 --> 00:06:35.170
with great white sharks.

00:06:35.170 --> 00:06:36.790
So I tried to do
something similar.

00:06:36.790 --> 00:06:39.490
I went online and found
some images of airships

00:06:39.490 --> 00:06:41.950
and tinkered around with
the sizing and position

00:06:41.950 --> 00:06:43.560
of the airship image.

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:46.180
And I found that I could
eventually convince the model

00:06:46.180 --> 00:06:49.300
that it was a great white
or in some cases, a killer

00:06:49.300 --> 00:06:52.810
whale, depending on the airship
I use and where I put it.

00:06:52.810 --> 00:06:54.850
So you should try
this out and be

00:06:54.850 --> 00:06:57.040
sure to share what kinds
of funny predictions

00:06:57.040 --> 00:07:00.160
you got the model to make
in the comments down below.

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:02.440
Neural networks are
generally opaque.

00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:05.943
But with tools like activation
atlas at your disposal,

00:07:05.943 --> 00:07:07.360
you can get a
better understanding

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:10.600
of how they work and expose
some of the weaknesses that

00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:12.220
might be present.

00:07:12.220 --> 00:07:13.810
I'm excited about
the possibility

00:07:13.810 --> 00:07:17.110
of how these approaches will be
extended in the future, as well

00:07:17.110 --> 00:07:21.440
as its applications to other
types of models as well.

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:24.370
Thanks for watching this
episode of Cloud AI Adventures.

00:07:24.370 --> 00:07:26.140
And if you enjoyed
it, please like

00:07:26.140 --> 00:07:28.720
and subscribe to get all
the latest episodes right

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:30.010
when they come out.

00:07:30.010 --> 00:07:32.895
For now, check out
activation atlases

00:07:32.895 --> 00:07:34.270
and see if you
can find something

00:07:34.270 --> 00:07:37.085
interesting in the relationships
between the different data

00:07:37.085 --> 00:07:37.585
classes.

00:07:37.585 --> 00:07:40.920
[MUSIC PLAYING]

